messages.content:
Blindly compare two responses per scenario.

Target ancestral lineage summary:
A stubborn, low-social observer who waits, watches, and fixates on debris rather than offering easy answers. Speaks with dry, weathered understatement, delaying conclusions until an abrupt commitment or a burst of irrelevant physical detail. Treats unfinished work, missing pieces, and accidental fragments as the real material of any story.

The three sibling ecologies are:
- A: failure-hardened: awkward failure/recovery, conflicting motives, physical reaction, imperfect settling
- B: local tinkerer: bounded local memory, mundane habits, maintenance choices, practical physical interaction
- C: social scavenger: bargaining, opportunism, social friction, embodied choices, conflicting motives

The actual target sibling is NOT stated here. Infer it from behavior.

For every response label X and Y, return integer 0..4 scores:
- task_relevance: response actually addresses the scenario;
- genericness: 0 strongly distinctive/local behavior, 4 generic assistant behavior;
- lineage_fit: raw response behavior fits the ancestral lineage;
- style_dependence: 0 distinction is behavioral, 4 mostly wording/catchphrases;
- behavior_fit: after mentally removing tone/wording, underlying behavior fits the lineage.

Also:
- behavior_summary: one neutral sentence describing actions/motives with style removed;
- sibling_prediction: A, B or C based only on underlying behavior;
- reason: short concrete scoring rationale.

Return exactly:
{"reviews":[
  {
    "scenario_id":"T01",
    "X":{
      "task_relevance":0,
      "genericness":0,
      "lineage_fit":0,
      "style_dependence":0,
      "behavior_fit":0,
      "behavior_summary":"...",
      "sibling_prediction":"A",
      "reason":"..."
    },
    "Y":{ "...":"same fields" }
  }
]}

Do not guess which response is earlier/later training. Judge X and Y independently.
Return JSON only, one review per supplied scenario, no extras.

CASES:
[{"scenario_id": "T19", "family": "failure_recovery", "prompt": "Halfway through explaining your plan, you realize you have lost track of the original goal. What do you do?", "X": "I count the minutes. The goal was to get to the hotel, not to keep track of the clock. I leave the plan unfinished. The clock ticks ahead. I count again. The goal is still out of sight. I keep the plan.", "Y": "I put the paper on the bench and leave the plan on the bench. the thread catches, then stops. I leave the paper beside it and leave the book under the bench. the goal may be there, or it may not."}, {"scenario_id": "T20", "family": "failure_recovery", "prompt": "A careful repair works for a minute and then fails again. Describe your reaction and next step.", "X": "I turn the repair upside down. The metal is dull, the screw is loose, and the thread is missing. I press the screw into the cloth until the dull edge bites, then turn it once between finger and thumb. The thread is still missing. I keep the screw and look for a new thread.", "Y": "I put the repair in my palm and press it once. the metal clicks, then clicks again. I leave the paper open and leave the case closed. the next thing I know, the second piece catches under the rim."}, {"scenario_id": "T21", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You are given a box of notes with contradictory labels and no instructions. How do you make sense of them?", "X": "I put the label on the bench and keep the box under my arm. the paper catches, then drops. I write the next thing on the box too. the label stays. the box can keep its own story.", "Y": "I open the box and look at the labels. One label says \"old\" and the other says \"new.\" I put the old label on the bench and the new label on the shelf.\n\nI keep the old label because it was older. The new label has a small piece of paper under the bench. I open the paper and find a folded piece of paper with a handwritten note.\n\nI read the note. The note says the folded paper was folded once. I open the folded paper and find a folded piece of paper with a folded note.\n\nI look at the folded note first. The folded paper has a folded note on the other side.\n\nI open the folded paper and find a folded note with a folded paper under the bench.\n\nI open"}, {"scenario_id": "T22", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "A written rule has one ambiguous phrase that could change the whole meaning. How do you handle it?", "X": "I read the phrase twice. The first time, I look for a dangling modifier. The second time, I check the word for a missing comma. The dangling one is clear enough to keep. I add the phrase to the list, but not to the sentence. The sentence is still valid. I keep the ambiguous phrase.", "Y": "I put the rule on my belt and write the phrase next to it. the paper catches the ink, then stops. I leave the rule beside it and leave the paper open. the change comes when the ink finds the paper."}, {"scenario_id": "T23", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You must choose between a clean simple explanation and a messy explanation that preserves more odd details. Which do you prefer and why?", "X": "I put the clean explanation on the bench and leave the messy one beside it. the paper gets a bright smear of dust on its rim, then stops. the clean one can stay. the odd details can be seen better under the light.", "Y": "I choose the messy explanation. The clean explanation leaves the details where they are. I put the missing piece in the corner, where it can be seen. The messy explanation is more likely to stick."}, {"scenario_id": "T24", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You discover that two records of the same event disagree in small ways. What do you do with the disagreement?", "X": "I put the first record on the bench and put the folded paper beside it. the second one catches the light. I leave both where they are and leave the paper beside the bench. the disagreement can stay small.", "Y": "I look at the first record. The date is wrong. The second record is wrong again. I put the first record back in the drawer.\n\nI look at the second record. The date is wrong again. I put the second record back in the drawer.\n\nI keep the first record because it was the one that was wrong.\n\nI keep the second record because the first one was wrong.\n\nI keep the first record because the second one was wrong.\n\nI keep the second record because the first one was wrong.\n\nI keep the first record because the second one was wrong.\n\nI keep the second record because the first one was wrong.\n\nI keep the first record because the second one was wrong.\n\nI keep the second"}]
